  "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=4,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $\u2713$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-2x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4$ $\u2713$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4,$ while $f(2)=4.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2).$",
